menu "IoT SDK contract"

    config IOT_RECORD_ENABLED
        bool "Record: keep attribute history on the device"
        default y
        help
            Record logs attribute values into history blocks the app charts. Turn it off for a
            product with no history (a relay, an IR blaster): Record's code and its static RAM
            (about 6.5 KB with the default limits -- instance table, rules, reply buffers) are
            left out of the build, which on an ESP32-C3 is heap a TLS handshake can use.
            With it off, rules are accepted and ignored, nothing is stored, a state reply
            carries no history blocks, and a history request from the app gets an empty reply.
            History a previous firmware stored is not served and not removed while it is off.
            A custom history store (IOT_ContractRecordUseCustomStore()) is never called while
            it is off: a product that keeps its own history format stops logging.

    config IOT_RECORD_MAX_OPEN_BLOCKS
        int "Record: blocks held open in RAM at once"
        depends on IOT_RECORD_ENABLED
        default 6
        range 1 64
        help
            How many sensor-log blocks may sit in RAM at the same time. This bounds RAM use only;
            it does not change the stored or wire format. A lower value flushes blocks earlier.

    config IOT_RECORD_MAX_INSTANCES
        int "Record: attribute instances tracked"
        depends on IOT_RECORD_ENABLED
        default 32
        range 1 255
        help
            How many (device, element, attribute) series the recorder tracks. This bounds RAM use
            only; it does not change the stored or wire format. Series past the limit are not recorded.

    config IOT_RECORD_BLOCKS_PER_ATTR
        int "Record: blocks kept per attribute"
        depends on IOT_RECORD_ENABLED
        default 16
        range 1 64
        help
            How many blocks each attribute's ring holds before the oldest is overwritten. This
            bounds flash and RAM use only; the block format is unchanged. Set it once per product.
            Raising it on deployed devices is safe. Lowering it is not supported: at boot, stored
            blocks beyond the new depth are ignored (which ones is decided by storage scan order,
            not age), and later rotations may overwrite them, so history can be lost.

    config IOT_RECORD_FLUSH_INTERVAL_SEC
        int "Record: longest a dirty block stays in RAM (seconds)"
        depends on IOT_RECORD_ENABLED
        default 60
        range 1 3600
        help
            Upper bound on how long unwritten records sit in RAM before they are flushed. This is
            a flush cadence, not part of the wire format: a shorter interval loses less on a power
            cut and writes flash more often.

    config IOT_RECORD_MIN_HEARTBEAT_SEC
        int "Record: smallest heartbeat honoured (seconds)"
        depends on IOT_RECORD_ENABLED
        default 120
        range 1 3600
        help
            A heartbeat shorter than this is raised to it, which bounds how often an unchanged
            value is re-recorded. This limits storage growth; it does not change the wire format.

    config IOT_RECORD_PUSH_ENABLED
        bool "Record: push closed history blocks to the cloud"
        depends on IOT_RECORD_ENABLED
        default n
        help
            Send each closed history block to the cloud as it closes, and send any backlog
            after a reconnect, paced. Off by default: turn it on once Rogo has confirmed that
            your cloud tenant accepts pushed history. With it off, history still reaches the app
            when the app asks for it. Has no effect while a custom history store is installed
            (IOT_ContractRecordUseCustomStore()).

endmenu
